                    <p class="section-lead mx-auto">
                        Because overseas buyers cannot inspect construction milestones in person, real estate promoters frequently exploit their absence through deliberate contractual breaches.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="row g-4">
                    <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-4">
                        <div class="legal-card">
                            <div class="legal-card-icon"><i class="fas fa-clock-rotate-left"></i></div>
                            <h3 class="legal-card-title">Inordinate Possession Delays</h3>
                            <p>
                                Multi-year construction stalls beyond the promised contractual completion date, accompanied by repeated unilateral extensions without statutory sanction or compensation.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-4">
                        <div class="legal-card">
                            <div class="legal-card-icon"><i class="fas fa-pen-ruler"></i></div>
                            <h3 class="legal-card-title">Unilateral Plan Modifications</h3>
                            <p>
                                Promoters altering master layouts, reducing promised carpet area, eliminating green belts, or adding unauthorized floors without securing the mandatory two-thirds allottee consent under Section 14(2).
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-4">
                        <div class="legal-card">
                            <div class="legal-card-icon"><i class="fas fa-receipt"></i></div>
                            <h3 class="legal-card-title">Unjustified Financial Demands</h3>
                            <p>
                                Imposing unlawful escalation charges, arbitrary infrastructure levies, or unilateral advance maintenance charges not stipulated in the registered Agreement for Sale.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="col-md-6 col-lg-4">
                        <div class="legal-card">
                            <div class="legal-card-icon"><i class="fas fa-triangle-exclamation"></i></div>
                            <h3 class="legal-card-title">Structural &amp; Quality Defects</h3>
                            <p>
                                Handing over flats with severe seepage, substandard electrical fixtures, cracks, or defective elevator systems, followed by refusal to honor the statutory 5-year defect liability warranty.
                            </p>
                        </div>
