        <!-- =========================================================
             5. DELAYED CONSTRUCTION & POSSESSION (SECTION 18)
             ========================================================== -->
        <section class="content-section" id="delayed-possession">
            <div class="container">
                <div class="row align-items-center g-5">
                    <div class="col-lg-6 order-lg-2">
                        <span class="section-eyebrow">Section 18 Enforcement</span>
                        <h2 class="section-h2">Delayed Possession Remedies: Refund vs. Monthly Interest</h2>
                        <p>
                            Under <strong>Section 18(1) of the RERA Act, 2016</strong>, when a promoter fails to complete or give possession of an apartment, plot, or building in accordance with the terms of the Agreement for Sale, the allottee is granted an absolute statutory choice:
                        </p>
                        <ul class="list-unstyled mb-4">
                            <li class="mb-3 d-flex align-items-start">
                                <i class="fas fa-check-circle text-gold me-2 mt-1"></i>
                                <div>
                                    <strong>Option A: Withdrawal &amp; 100% Principal Refund with Interest:</strong> If the allottee elects to exit the stalled project, the promoter is legally bound to return the entire consideration paid, together with statutory interest calculated at SBI highest MCLR + 2% per annum from the date of each deposit until realization.
                                </div>
                            </li>
                            <li class="mb-3 d-flex align-items-start">
                                <i class="fas fa-check-circle text-gold me-2 mt-1"></i>
                                <div>
                                    <strong>Option B: Continued Allotment with Monthly Delay Interest:</strong> If the allottee elects to retain the property, the promoter must pay monthly compensatory interest for every single month of delay from the promised delivery date until actual physical handover with an Occupancy Certificate.
                                </div>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>
