        <section class="content-section" id="faq">
            <div class="container">
                <div class="text-center mb-4">
                    <span class="section-eyebrow">Clarity on Law &amp; Procedure</span>
                    <h2 class="section-h2">Frequently Asked Questions on NRI RERA &amp; Builder Disputes</h2>
                    <p class="section-lead mx-auto">
                        Authoritative legal insights into RERA litigation, delayed possession remedies, refund entitlements, and remote dispute resolution in Tamil Nadu.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="row justify-content-center">
                    <div class="col-lg-10">
                        <div class="accordion" id="reraFaqAccordion">

                            <!-- FAQ 1 -->
                            <div class="accordion-item">
                                <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingOne">
                                    <button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseOne" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="collapseOne">
                                        1. Can an NRI file a RERA complaint against a builder without traveling to India?
                                    </button>
                                </h3>
                                <div id="collapseOne" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" aria-labelledby="headingOne" data-bs-parent="#reraFaqAccordion">
                                    <div class="accordion-body">
                                        Yes, absolutely. Non-Resident Indians (NRIs) and Overseas Citizens of India (OCIs) can institute and pursue RERA proceedings entirely remotely. The NRI executes a Special Power of Attorney (PoA) in favor of their advocate or trusted local representative, attested by the Indian Embassy or Consulate abroad or apostilled under the Hague Convention, and adjudicated before the District Registrar in Tamil Nadu. Our advocates represent the NRI before the Tamil Nadu Real Estate Regulatory Authority (TNRERA) and Appellate Tribunal in Chennai.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <!-- FAQ 2 -->
                            <div class="accordion-item">
                                <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingTwo">
                                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseTwo" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseTwo">
                                        2. What statutory interest rate can an NRI claim for delayed possession under TNRERA Rules?
                                    </button>
                                </h3>
                                <div id="collapseTwo" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingTwo" data-bs-parent="#reraFaqAccordion">
                                    <div class="accordion-body">
                                        Under Rule 18 of the Tamil Nadu Real Estate (Regulation and Development) Rules, 2017, the statutory rate of interest payable by the promoter to the allottee on delayed possession is the State Bank of India (SBI) Highest Marginal Cost of Lending Rate (MCLR) plus two percent (2%). This monthly interest accrues from the contractual date of promised possession until the actual physical handover of the apartment with a valid Completion/Occupancy Certificate.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <!-- FAQ 3 -->
                            <div class="accordion-item">
                                <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingThree">
                                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseThree" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseThree">
                                        3. Can an NRI choose between withdrawing from the project for a refund or waiting for possession with interest?
                                    </button>
                                </h3>
                                <div id="collapseThree" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingThree" data-bs-parent="#reraFaqAccordion">
                                    <div class="accordion-body">
                                        Yes. Under Section 18(1) of the Real Estate (Regulation and Development) Act, 2016, the allottee possesses an unqualified statutory election. If the promoter fails to hand over possession by the date specified in the Agreement for Sale, the allottee can either: (1) withdraw completely from the project and demand an immediate 100% refund of all deposited amounts along with prescribed statutory interest and compensation, or (2) remain in the project and receive monthly interest from the builder for every month of delay until actual handover.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <!-- FAQ 4 -->
                            <div class="accordion-item">
                                <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingFour">
                                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseFour" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseFour">
                                        4. What is the difference between Form M and Form N under TNRERA?
                                    </button>
                                </h3>
                                <div id="collapseFour" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingFour" data-bs-parent="#reraFaqAccordion">
                                    <div class="accordion-body">
                                        Form M is filed under Section 31 before the Tamil Nadu Real Estate Regulatory Authority (TNRERA) for directions regarding statutory violations, project completion, handover of possession, or payment of interest on delay under Section 18. In contrast, Form N is filed under Section 71 before the Adjudicating Officer when the allottee seeks compensation and damages for financial loss, mental anguish, or misrepresentation under Sections 12, 14, 18, and 19.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <!-- FAQ 5 -->
                            <div class="accordion-item">
                                <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingFive">
                                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseFive" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseFive">
                                        5. Can a builder change the architectural layout, floor plan, or amenities without buyer consent?
                                    </button>
                                </h3>
                                <div id="collapseFive" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingFive" data-bs-parent="#reraFaqAccordion">
                                    <div class="accordion-body">
                                        No. Under Section 14(2) of the RERA Act, a promoter cannot make minor additions or alterations to an individual apartment without the buyer's prior consent, and cannot make any structural modifications or additions to the overall sanctioned building plan or common areas without the previous written consent of at least two-thirds (2/3) of the allottees who have agreed to take apartments in that building.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <!-- FAQ 6 -->
                            <div class="accordion-item">
                                <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingSix">
                                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseSix" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseSix">
                                        6. What is the 5-year structural defect warranty under RERA Section 14(3)?
                                    </button>
                                </h3>
                                <div id="collapseSix" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingSix" data-bs-parent="#reraFaqAccordion">
                                    <div class="accordion-body">
                                        Section 14(3) of the RERA Act establishes a statutory five-year defect liability warranty. If any structural defect or other defect in workmanship, quality, or provision of services is brought to the notice of the promoter within five years from the date of handing over possession, the promoter is legally obligated to rectify the defect without further charge within thirty (30) days. If the promoter fails to do so, the aggrieved allottee is entitled to seek compensation through the Authority.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <!-- FAQ 7 -->
                            <div class="accordion-item">
                                <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingSeven">
                                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseSeven" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseSeven">
                                        7. Can an NRI approach both RERA and the Consumer Commission (NCDRC/SCDRC)?
                                    </button>
                                </h3>
                                <div id="collapseSeven" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingSeven" data-bs-parent="#reraFaqAccordion">
                                    <div class="accordion-body">
                                        The Supreme Court of India in landmark judgments including <em>Imperia Structures Ltd. v. Anil Patni (2020)</em> and <em>Experion Developers Pvt. Ltd. v. Sushma Ashok Shiroor (2022)</em> affirmed that remedies under the RERA Act and the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 are concurrent and complementary. An allottee has the legal right to elect either forum based on strategic considerations such as project registration status, claim quantum, and relief sought.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <!-- FAQ 8 -->
                            <div class="accordion-item">
                                <h3 class="accordion-header" id="headingEight">
                                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapseEight" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="collapseEight">
                                        8. How are TNRERA recovery orders and decrees executed against defaulting builders?
                                    </button>
                                </h3>
                                <div id="collapseEight" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="headingEight" data-bs-parent="#reraFaqAccordion">
                                    <div class="accordion-body">
                                        Under Section 40 of the RERA Act and Rule 26 of the Tamil Nadu RERA Rules, any refund, interest, or compensation awarded by the Authority or Adjudicating Officer that remains unpaid is enforceable as arrears of land revenue. The Authority issues a Recovery Warrant forwarded to the jurisdictional District Collector, who initiates revenue recovery proceedings, attachment of builder bank accounts, and auction of promoter properties.
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
